// src/widget_kind.pdx -- postui.M1-007 (paideia-os/postui#7)
//
// WidgetKind module: closed sum type over all 15 widget kinds +
// widget_draw dispatch function.
//
// =============================================================================
// SCOPE (docs/design.md §1.2, §2.6 widget catalog, §5.1 M1-007)
// =============================================================================
//
// This module is the postui analogue of the kernel's cap-handler
// dispatch pattern (cap_handler_tui_canvas.pdx / cap_handler_tty.pdx):
// one entry function that takes an ordinal + argument tuple, cmp/je-
// chains over the closed set of ordinals, and forwards each match to
// the appropriate handler body. The design doc's §1.2 architectural
// resolution ("closed WidgetKind enum + widget_draw dispatch, no trait
// objects, no vtables") is realized verbatim by this file.
//
// The M1-001 scaffold seeded the widget-ordinal ID constants
// (WK_BLOCK..WK_CLEAR, WK_COUNT) so downstream code could key against
// them for logging / stat counters / boot-smoke assertions without
// waiting for the M1-007 body. This landing preserves every WK_*
// value verbatim (semantic identity of an ordinal never changes once
// shipped -- adding widget #16 in a future release requires bumping
// POSTUI_VERSION_MINOR and reserving a fresh ordinal past the current
// top; the scaffold's own header pins this contract).
//
// =============================================================================
// WHAT LANDS AT M1-007
// =============================================================================
//
//   1. Two error sentinels in the postui error band (0xFFFFEB80+):
//        WK_ERR_UNIMPLEMENTED (0xFFFFEBB0): the kind ordinal is in
//          [0, WK_COUNT) but no v1 widget body exists for it -- the
//          14 non-Block kinds all fall here until their M2..M4 landings.
//        WK_ERR_BAD_KIND (0xFFFFEBB1): the kind ordinal is >= WK_COUNT.
//          A well-formed caller never triggers this (WidgetKind is
//          closed; the type-checker would reject a WK_* constant that
//          did not exist), so this is a defensive gate against a
//          hand-crafted numeric literal or memory-corrupted frame.
//
//   2. widget_draw(kind, spec, buf, rows, cols) -> u64: the one entry
//      every Frame::draw call goes through. Compare-chain dispatch:
//      bounds-check kind < WK_COUNT, then a single cmp/je for the one
//      implemented ordinal (WK_BLOCK=0), else fall through to the
//      unimplemented sentinel. Every M2..M4 widget-body landing adds
//      exactly one cmp/je + one call target here -- the same edit-
//      locality the kernel cap-dispatch tables have kept intact
//      through eight+ cap kinds.
//
// =============================================================================
// DISPATCH SHAPE: COMPARE-CHAIN vs FUNCTION-POINTER TABLE
// =============================================================================
//
// The obvious alternative to a compare-chain is a `[u64; 15]` table of
// function pointers indexed by `kind`, jumped through with an indirect
// call. That posture has three concrete costs at M1-007's actual state:
//
//   (1) paideia-as's `pub let X : [u64; N] = { ... }` data-initializer
//       does not accept function-symbol literals (see the paideia-as
//       encoder pitfalls memory-note). Building the table thus needs a
//       runtime widget_kind_init() call, adding a boot-order dependency
//       between whoever calls widget_draw and the init sequencing --
//       exactly the class of hidden state Frame::init tries to avoid.
//   (2) The perf argument does not survive scrutiny at 15 branches.
//       Modern branch predictors classify a compare-chain of <=16
//       entries as first-touch predictable; an indirect call through a
//       non-hot memory location adds one D$ miss the compare-chain
//       does not have.
//   (3) At v1 only 1 of 15 targets is implemented. A 15-slot table
//       would be 14 slots pointing at the same stub function -- more
//       LOC and more indirection than the two-line cmp/je body that
//       dispatches the one real handler and falls through to the stub
//       return.
//
// A table becomes attractive around ~5+ implemented kinds when the
// cmp/je chain starts to bunch up in the icache. M2 lands Paragraph,
// List, Table, Tabs, Scrollbar -- at that point the table+init pair
// becomes the right shape and a rewrite here is a mechanical exercise
// (introduce widget_kind_init, populate 15 slots, replace widget_draw
// body with a bounds-check + indirect call). This landing does not
// pre-invest in that infrastructure.
//
// =============================================================================
// PAIDEIA-AS CONFORMANCE
// =============================================================================
//
//   - Module name PascalCase basename (`WidgetKind`), matches file
//     basename per manifest.pdxproj sources: entry.
//   - No `test` mnemonic; the one bounds check is `cmp reg, imm32`
//     (WK_COUNT = 15 fits imm32 trivially).
//   - Error sentinels 0xFFFFEBB0 and 0xFFFFEBB1 both exceed
//     0x7FFFFFFF, so `mov rax, <sentinel>` is emitted by paideia-as as
//     movabs (10-byte imm64 form) -- legal for mov, matches the
//     cap_handler_tui_canvas.pdx `mov rax, 0xFFFFEB1A` idiom
//     verbatim (see kernel-side R89.M1-002).
//   - No 2-op `imul reg, imm`; no imm64 in cmp/and; no r11 preserved
//     across the nested call (r11 is scratch per paideia-as posture).
//   - widget_draw is a non-leaf: it makes one nested call to
//     block_draw for the WK_BLOCK arm. SysV alignment discipline
//     applies: entry rsp%16==8; `sub rsp, 8` before the call rounds
//     to 0; `add rsp, 8` after restores; `ret` pops the return
//     address (unchanged). No callee-save register is written -- the
//     four argument shuffles land in caller-save rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx, all
//     of which are already dead across the return.
//   - Effect/capability row `!{mem} @{}` -- widget_draw's transitive
//     effect set is a superset of block_draw's (which is !{mem} @{}).
//     No widget in the closed set introduces sysreg or cap effects at
//     v1 (see docs/design.md §2.3 -- widget bodies write cells and
//     pack semantic-pipe records, both mem-only; the actual pipe send
//     is deferred to M2-009 and does not run inside widget_draw).
//   - Labels prefixed `wk_` per docs/design.md fingerprint discipline.

module WidgetKind = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // Widget kind ordinals (docs/design.md §2.6 catalog order).
  // Assigned to a fixed closed set at v1; adding a new widget at a
  // future release requires bumping POSTUI_VERSION_MINOR and reserving
  // a fresh ordinal past the current top -- an existing ordinal never
  // changes semantic identity.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let WK_BLOCK       : u64 = 0
  pub let WK_PARAGRAPH   : u64 = 1
  pub let WK_LIST        : u64 = 2
  pub let WK_TABLE       : u64 = 3
  pub let WK_TABS        : u64 = 4
  pub let WK_GAUGE       : u64 = 5
  pub let WK_SPARKLINE   : u64 = 6
  pub let WK_BARCHART    : u64 = 7
  pub let WK_CHART       : u64 = 8
  pub let WK_CANVAS      : u64 = 9
  pub let WK_TREE        : u64 = 10
  pub let WK_CALENDAR    : u64 = 11
  pub let WK_SCROLLBAR   : u64 = 12
  pub let WK_TEXTINPUT   : u64 = 13
  pub let WK_CLEAR       : u64 = 14

  // Cardinality of the widget catalog at v1. Locked at 15 to match
  // docs/design.md §1.2 example (15 variants). A dispatch table's
  // static-array reservation keys on this constant so a future WK_*
  // addition is caught by a compile-time size check rather than
  // silently over-indexing an out-of-date table.
  pub let WK_COUNT : u64 = 15

  // ==========================================================================
  // Error sentinels (postui error band, 0xFFFFEB80+).
  //
  // WK_ERR_UNIMPLEMENTED: kind ordinal is in [0, WK_COUNT) but no
  //   drawable body exists for it at v1. The 14 non-Block kinds
  //   return this until their M2..M4 landings ship a real body.
  //   Callers can key on this specific sentinel to render a
  //   placeholder cell (e.g. an inverse-video "?" glyph) rather than
  //   abort the whole frame.
  //
  // WK_ERR_BAD_KIND: kind ordinal is >= WK_COUNT. The type-checker
  //   already prevents a well-formed WidgetKind reference from
  //   landing here, so hitting it means a hand-crafted numeric
  //   literal or a memory-corrupted frame. Distinct from
  //   UNIMPLEMENTED so a downstream reader can tell "we know that
  //   widget, just not yet" from "that's not a widget at all".
  //
  // Both sentinels sit in the postui error band (Lib::POSTUI_ERR_
  // BAND_BASE = 0xFFFFEB80) so a downstream >> 8 discriminator
  // attributes the return to postui vs. the kernel TCC band
  // (0xFFFFEB10..1F) or the KIND_SIG_KEY band (0xFFFFEB00..0F).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let WK_ERR_UNIMPLEMENTED : u64 = 0xFFFFEBB0
  pub let WK_ERR_BAD_KIND      : u64 = 0xFFFFEBB1

  // ==========================================================================
  // widget_draw(kind, spec_ptr, buf_ptr, rows, cols) -> u64
  //
  // The one entry every Frame::draw call routes through. Dispatches on
  // `kind` to the appropriate per-widget draw body; returns the body's
  // own return value (0 = OK for the Block v1 body, per block.pdx's
  // documented contract). For unknown/unimplemented kinds returns one
  // of the WK_ERR_* sentinels above.
  //
  // Signature matches every per-widget draw body one-for-one after the
  // kind ordinal is stripped: (spec_ptr, buf_ptr, rows, cols) -> u64.
  // The dispatch shifts the four widget-side args down one slot
  // (rsi -> rdi, rdx -> rsi, rcx -> rdx, r8 -> rcx) before the nested
  // call, exactly as SysV would do for a five-arg function calling a
  // four-arg function. Each future widget body (paragraph_draw,
  // list_draw, ...) plugs in with two lines of edit here: one
  // cmp/je above the existing WK_BLOCK arm, one call target below
  // -- the same edit-locality shape cap_handler_tui_canvas's tuih_op_*
  // dispatch has maintained through eight ops.
  //
  // Register plan:
  //   Args: rdi=kind, rsi=spec_ptr, rdx=buf_ptr, rcx=rows, r8=cols.
  //   Bounds check: `cmp rdi, 15` (imm32-safe), `jae wk_wd_bad_kind`.
  //   Dispatch: single `cmp rdi, 0` + `je wk_wd_block` for WK_BLOCK;
  //     falls through to the unimplemented arm otherwise.
  //   WK_BLOCK arm: shift args (rsi->rdi, rdx->rsi, rcx->rdx, r8->rcx)
  //     via four mov reg,reg; `sub rsp, 8` to align rsp%16 from the
  //     entry-time 8 to the call-safe 0; `call block_draw` returns its
  //     u64 in rax verbatim; `add rsp, 8` restores; `ret`.
  //   Unimplemented arm: `mov rax, 0xFFFFEBB0` (paideia-as emits
  //     10-byte movabs since imm > 0x7FFFFFFF); `ret`.
  //   Bad-kind arm: `mov rax, 0xFFFFEBB1`; `ret`.
  //
  // Non-leaf (one nested call). Alignment: entry rsp%16==8; `sub rsp,
  // 8` -> 0 at every nested call site (only the WK_BLOCK arm has
  // one). No callee-save register touched (the four arg shuffles land
  // in caller-save rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx, all dead across the return).
  //
  // Effect/capability row: !{mem} @{}. widget_draw's transitive effect
  // set is a superset of every arm's; block_draw is !{mem} @{}, and
  // no future widget at v1 introduces sysreg or cap effects (the
  // semantic-pipe send lands separately at M2-009, not inside
  // widget_draw).
  //
  // Labels prefixed wk_ per docs/design.md fingerprint discipline.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let widget_draw : (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (kind: u64) (spec_ptr: u64) (buf_ptr: u64) (rows: u64) (cols: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem},
      capabilities: {},
      justification: "postui.M1-007 (postui#7): widget_draw is the compare-chain dispatch entry every Frame::draw call routes through -- the postui analogue of the kernel's cap_handler_tui_canvas / cap_handler_tty op dispatch, one-for-one on shape. Bounds check first (cmp rdi, 15; jae wk_wd_bad_kind) covers a hand-crafted or memory-corrupted kind ordinal at v1's max (WK_COUNT=15, imm32-safe). Then a single cmp/je for the one implemented ordinal (WK_BLOCK=0) routes to the WK_BLOCK arm; every other ordinal in [1, 15) falls through to the unimplemented sentinel. WK_BLOCK arm shifts the four widget-side args down one SysV slot (rsi->rdi spec_ptr, rdx->rsi buf_ptr, rcx->rdx rows, r8->rcx cols) via four mov reg,reg; sub rsp,8 aligns rsp%16 from entry-time 8 to 0 for the nested call; call block_draw (cross-module, resolved by the linker against block.pdx's pub let block_draw symbol) returns its u64 in rax which we pass through verbatim; add rsp,8 restores; ret. Unimplemented arm: mov rax, 0xFFFFEBB0 (paideia-as emits movabs since imm > 0x7FFFFFFF -- same idiom as cap_handler_tui_canvas.pdx's `mov rax, 0xFFFFEB1A` for TUI_BAD_SLOT); ret. Bad-kind arm: mov rax, 0xFFFFEBB1; ret. No callee-save touched (the four caller-save arg regs are dead across the return); no r11 preserved (paideia-as scratch); no `test` mnemonic; every immediate that participates in cmp/and (0, 15) fits imm32; the two sentinel movs go through mov reg, imm64 (movabs), legal for mov, not for cmp/and. Effect/capability row {mem}/{} covers block_draw's transitive shape verbatim (block_draw is also !{mem} @{}). Labels prefixed wk_ per docs fingerprint discipline (wk_wd_ for widget_draw arms).",
      block: {
        // ---- Bounds check: reject kind >= WK_COUNT ------------------------
        cmp rdi, 15;
        jae wk_wd_bad_kind;

        // ---- Dispatch: WK_BLOCK (0) -> block_draw -------------------------
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  wk_wd_block;

        // ---- Fall-through: kind is in [1, 15), no v1 body -----------------
        mov rax, 0xFFFFEBB0;
        ret;

      wk_wd_block:
        // Shift args down one slot: (spec, buf, rows, cols) -> block_draw.
        mov rdi, rsi;
        mov rsi, rdx;
        mov rdx, rcx;
        mov rcx, r8;

        // Align rsp for nested call (entry rsp%16==8 -> 0), call, restore.
        sub rsp, 8;
        call block_draw;
        add rsp, 8;
        ret;

      wk_wd_bad_kind:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFEBB1;
        ret;
      }
    }

}
